<details open>
  <summary>How to read this — how spin, release and arrival point connect</summary>
  <div class="body">
    <h3>1. The spin axis (clock) sets only the <i>direction</i> of the break</h3>
    12:00 breaks straight up (it resists gravity), 6:00 straight down (it falls faster than gravity alone), 3:00 toward the catcher's right, 9:00 toward the catcher's left.
    For a right-hander the arm side (third-base side, the catcher's left) sits around 8:00&ndash;11:00 and the glove side around 1:00&ndash;4:00. For a left-hander it is mirrored.
    <br><br>
    The thing to keep straight: <b>the clock value and the axis the ball is physically spinning about are 90&deg; apart</b>. A pure backspin ball (12:00) spins about a horizontal axis pointing along the 3&ndash;9 line.
    The blue rod drawn through the ball in section 1 is the real spin axis; the white arrow is the break that results from it.

    <h3>2. Spin efficiency (active spin) sets the <i>amount</i></h3>
    Only the component of spin perpendicular to the direction of travel makes Magnus force. The component parallel to travel is <b>gyro spin</b> — like a rifle bullet, it produces no break at all.
    <code>gyro angle = arccos(efficiency)</code>. This is the real difference between a slider and a curveball: both spin at 2400&ndash;2600 rpm, but a slider runs around 30% efficiency (almost all gyro) and a curveball around 75%.

    <h3>3. Spin rate only matters multiplied by efficiency</h3>
    What does the work is <code>spin rate &times; efficiency</code> (useful spin). 3000 rpm at 20% efficiency is 600 rpm of useful spin;
    2000 rpm at 90% is 1800. The second pitch breaks far more. "More spin = better pitch" is not true on its own.

    <h3>4. The arm slot moves both the spin axis and the arrival point</h3>
    The lower the arm, the more the spin axis tips sideways. As a rough rule,
    <code>spin axis (clock, catcher view) = 12:00 &minus; (90&deg; &minus; arm slot) &divide; 30&deg;</code>.
    For a right-hander: over the top is 12:00, high three-quarters (about 60&deg;) is 11:00, sidearm (about 15&deg;) is around 9:30.
    Tick "Derive the spin axis from the arm slot too" and the axis follows the slot on that relationship.
    At the same time the <b>release drops and moves out to the arm side</b>, so the same aim lands somewhere else horizontally.
    <br><br>
    Breaking balls are a different story. The axis is created by the grip and how the fingers come off the ball, so it departs a long way from whatever the arm action would give on its own.
    Only fastballs really follow "slot = axis".

    <h3>5. The aim and the arrival point are not the same place</h3>
    "Aim" on this page means <b>the point the ball would reach with no spin at all</b>. The real ball ends up offset from it by exactly its break.
    A right-hander's four-seam lands above the aim and to the arm side. What a pitcher is actually doing is
    <b>allowing for that offset</b>. The "Land it middle-middle" button solves it backwards for you.
    It will tell you, in inches, that a sweeper has to be aimed 20" to the arm side to finish middle-middle, or that a curveball has to be aimed well above the top of the zone.

    <h3>6. VAA (vertical approach angle) — where release height earns its keep</h3>
    The angle to the horizontal at the moment the ball crosses the plate. Negative is downward.
    A four-seam around &minus;4&deg; is "flat", which is thought to be hard to match with a swing plane up in the zone, hence the whiffs.
    <b>For the same arrival point, a higher release gives a steeper angle and a lower release a flatter one.</b> The "By release height" table shows it.
    <br><br>
    But <b>dropping the arm slot can make VAA steeper, not flatter</b>. Lowering the arm tips the axis sideways and kills the induced vertical break,
    and the extra drop outweighs the lower release. Turn on "Derive the spin axis from the arm slot too" in the "By arm slot" table and you can watch that reversal happen.

    <h3>7. Where you stand on the rubber</h3>
    The pitcher's plate is <b>24 in &times; 6 in</b>, so the edges are 12 inches either side of centre.
    The pivot foot only has to be in contact with it, which leaves close to 20 inches of usable width to choose from.
    Move along the rubber and <b>the release point moves sideways with you, one for one</b>.
    <br><br>
    The important part: <b>the break does not change at all</b>. Same spin, same velocity, same Magnus force —
    IVB and HB do not move by a single inch. Three things do change.
    <br><br>
    &bull; <b>Horizontal approach angle (HAA)</b>. To reach the same location, moving the release a foot sideways changes the angle by about 1.0&deg; over the 54-foot flight.<br>
    &bull; <b>How far the ball crosses the plate</b>. Stand on the arm-side edge and throw to the opposite corner and the ball cuts diagonally across in front of the hitter.
    A right-hander on the third-base edge gets a much sharper "coming in from behind" angle on a right-handed batter — crossfire.
    That is exactly why sidearmers live on the extreme arm-side edge.<br>
    &bull; <b>Where the ball appears from</b> relative to the hitter's eyes. Harder to pick up for same-handed batters, easier for opposite-handed ones — it cuts both ways.
    <br><br>
    Section 3's <b>"By rubber spot"</b> sweeps the stance with the arrival point pinned to the middle of the zone.
    One caveat: <b>Rapsodo's Release Side is measured from the centre line of the plate</b>, so the stance is already baked into it.
    On a profile imported from CSV, leave the rubber spot at 0 and move it only to ask "what if he moved over".

    <h3>8. Extension</h3>
    How far in front of the rubber the ball is released. The further out, the shorter the trip to the hitter, so the same velocity arrives sooner and <i>plays</i> faster.
    The relationship is <code>perceived velocity = velocity &times; 54.0 ft &divide; (60.5 ft &minus; extension)</code>,
    so releasing a foot further out than league average (about 6.5 ft) is worth roughly +1 mph.

    <h3>9. Pitch tunnels</h3>
    The hitter has to decide whether to swing long before the ball gets there. The swing itself takes about 175 ms,
    so on an average fastball the decision is already made <b>23.8 feet in front of home plate</b>.
    Baseball Prospectus calls this the <b>tunnel point</b> and anchors its tunnelling metrics on it.
    <br><br>
    So if two pitches are still in the same place at that point, the hitter cannot tell them apart.
    How far they separate <i>after</i> it is what produces whiffs and weak contact. In section 4's separation graph, look for
    <b>overlap up to the tunnel point and a sharp split after it</b>.
    A baseball is 2.9 inches across, so while the circles in the cross-section still overlap you can treat the two as looking like one pitch.
    <br><br>
    The biggest tunnel-breaker is <b>release point scatter</b>. If the arm action or arm position changes between pitch types,
    they start from different places and nothing you do with the break can save it.
    Move section 4's "Release scatter" by an inch or two and watch the tunnel fall apart.

    <h3>MLB classification is not a set of rules</h3>
    Statcast's pitch-type calls come from a model using velocity, break, spin, release position and the pitcher's whole repertoire, and they take the pitcher's own label into account.
    There are no mechanical thresholds, so the verdict in section 1 is <b>"how close is this to each MLB pitch type's average profile"</b>.
    Several types competing near a boundary is exactly what happens in real MLB data too — slider/cutter, slider/sweeper and curveball/slurve are continuous, not discrete.

    <h3>10. Learning to actually <i>see</i> spin</h3>
    Drop the playback in sections 2 and 4 to 0.05&ndash;0.10&times; and you can follow the seams on an incoming ball at 10&ndash;20&times; real time.
    The <b>spin loupe</b> (top right in section 2, below in section 4) blows the ball up to a fixed size and keeps it turning on the same axis,
    so you can learn "this is how that pitch spins" even though you could never see it at 60 feet.
    In section 4 the loupes for each pitch type sit side by side, which makes it obvious that <b>spin is the only cue the hitter is being given</b>.
    <br><br>
    Tick "Pause at the decision point" and it stops dead 23.8 feet short of the plate.
    What the ball looks like and how it is spinning at that instant is everything the hitter has. Press Throw again to let it finish.
    <br><br>
    At the real distance the seams are not visible, so turn up "Apparent ball size" to learn the spin,
    then put it back to 1.0&times; to get close to the real thing.
    <br><br>
    <b>1.00&times; is real time.</b> The flight time shown in section 2 and the wall-clock time the playback takes are the same (measured and confirmed).
    But at 60 fps there are only about 25 frames in a 0.4-second flight. A real hitter sees a continuous image, so raw frames
    would stutter and feel slower than reality. Each frame's motion is therefore split into several sub-steps
    drawn as faint trailing images. The <b>"Real speed (1.00&times;)"</b> button plays one pitch in that state.

    <h3>11. Pitcher profiles</h3>
    The tabs at the top are pitchers. A pitcher owns a <b>handedness, arm slot, release height and side, extension and rubber spot</b>,
    plus an <b>arsenal</b>. Switching tabs swaps all of it at once.
    The aim is saved per pitch too, so "this pitcher throws this pitch here" stays on the page.
    <br><br>
    The same pitch type is a different pitch from a different pitcher. Take one slider spin profile:
    over the top (6.27 ft release) versus sidearm (5.02 ft) changes VAA by more than 2&deg; and moves the arrival point a long way horizontally.
    Flip between the tabs and compare.
    <br><br>
    <b>On saving</b>: this page stores nothing in the browser (storage is not available in every environment).
    Export a profile to a file with "Export (.json)" and bring it back with "Import" next time.
    Import adds rather than replaces, so you can keep one file per pitcher and load whichever you need.

    <h3>12. Building an arsenal from measured numbers</h3>
    If you have Rapsodo, TrackMan or Statcast numbers in front of you, there is no need to hunt for the spin axis and efficiency with sliders.
    In <b>"Build the arsenal from measured numbers"</b>, type each pitch's <b>velocity, spin rate, vertical break and horizontal break</b>, tick "Measured",
    and the <b>spin axis and efficiency are solved backwards</b> to reproduce exactly that break. Everything downstream runs on those numbers.
    <br><br>
    <b>Signs</b>: vertical break (IVB) is positive upward, negative downward.
    Horizontal break (HB) is <b>positive to the arm side</b> — for a right-hander, movement toward the catcher's left (third-base side);
    for a left-hander, toward the catcher's right.
    Keeping it arm-relative means flipping RHP to LHP mirrors everything without retyping a number.
    Devices differ on sign and viewpoint, so do not copy the sign out of the file —
    set it from which way the pitch actually breaks. The spin axis display can also be switched between
    <b>catcher view and pitcher view (Rapsodo)</b>; Rapsodo's Tilt is pitcher view.
    <br><br>
    <b>"Not enough spin"</b> means that spin rate cannot physically produce the break you typed
    (the solved efficiency would exceed 100%). The rate you would need is shown, so either re-check the measured spin
    or accept that the pitch is breaking more than the spin alone explains — seam-shifted wake.
    This model does not look at seam orientation, so sinkers, splitters and changeups often measure more than they compute.
    <br><br>
    <b>&Delta;Height, &Delta;Side and &Delta;Ext</b> are that pitch's own release offset, in inches. Real pitchers release each pitch type an inch or two apart,
    and it changes the tunnel a great deal. Put the per-pitch differences from Rapsodo's release height and release side here.
    <br><br>
    <b>The MLB-average profile is left in place</b>, so you can hold a measured profile next to it and ask
    "where exactly does this pitcher's slider differ from league average".

    <h3>13. Importing a CSV</h3>
    Open <b>"Import an arsenal from a Rapsodo (or other) CSV"</b> at the top of the page to turn
    pitch-by-pitch data into a whole profile in one go.
    <br><br>
    <b>Column mapping</b> happens automatically, but column names vary by device and firmware version,
    so check it with your eyes. The names it looks for are
    Velocity / Total Spin / Spin Efficiency / Spin Direction / VB(trajectory) / HB(trajectory) /
    Release Height / Release Side / Release Extension / Pitch Type / Player Name.
    For break it <b>prefers the trajectory-based columns over the spin-based estimates</b>.
    <br><br>
    <b>Units</b> (mph/km-h, inches/cm, ft/m/cm) are guessed from the magnitudes and can be overridden by hand.
    <b>Aggregation</b> can be mean or median. If one wild pitch is sitting in the sample, median is the safer choice.
    <br><br>
    <b>The horizontal sign</b> is the one thing that cannot be decided automatically, because some devices use "arm side positive" and others "catcher's right positive".
    Throw one in section 2 after importing, and if it breaks the wrong way, tick "Horizontal sign is inverted" and import again.
    <br><br>
    <b>Release point</b>: the mean across all pitches becomes this pitcher's common release point, and each pitch type's
    difference from it goes in as &Delta;Height, &Delta;Side and &Delta;Ext. The arm slot is solved back from the height and side.
    Release <b>scatter (1&sigma;)</b> is computed too and can be fed into section 4's jitter, so how well the arm action actually repeats
    shows up directly in how the tunnel looks.
    <br><br>
    If you are unsure about the column names, press <b>"Export a template"</b> for a sample CSV and swap the contents.

    <h3>14. Tunnel search by purpose (section 5)</h3>
    Section 5 brute-forces "which two pitches, through which tunnel, to do this particular job".
    It lays a grid across the decision point, solves each pitch's release angle to pass through each point, computes the arrival point, and scores it against the goal.
    <br><br>
    <b>Strikeout (two strikes)</b>: the setup pitch is a strike, the finisher misses the zone by 4&ndash;10 inches. With the hitter put away, a bigger miss still gets swung at.<br>
    <b>Strike when behind</b>: both pitches are strikes. You cannot afford a worse count, so it looks for pairs where either one lands in the zone. Indistinguishable, so neither gets sat on.<br>
    <b>Chase when behind</b>: the setup pitch is a strike, the finisher misses the zone by <b>only 1&ndash;5 inches</b>. The window the hitter will chase is narrow, so it wants a near miss that is still a borderline call if taken.<br>
    <b>Weak contact</b>: both pitches down. It looks for a relationship where the bat gets there but not the barrel.
    <br><br>
    <b>The release-angle gap</b> is what matters. Two pitches can be made to pass through the same point, but if getting them there requires a different arm angle or release,
    the hitter reads it out of the hand. Under 1.5&deg; is comfortably inside the same arm action;
    over 2.5&deg; cannot be done without changing the arm. Section 5 marks that gap down in the score.

    <h3>Notes on using measured data</h3>
    Rapsodo Pro 2.0 <b>does not measure extension</b>. Measure the stride distance from the rubber with a tape and type it into section 2 yourself.
    Both perceived velocity and where the tunnel forms move by a foot or more on that number alone.
    Rapsodo's Release Angle is the angle <b>at release</b> and is a different quantity from the VAA this app reports (the angle at the plate).
    For break, the <b>trajectory-based</b> figures are closer to reality than the spin-based estimates.
    In very different temperature or altitude, the same spin produces a few percent more or less break (this model fixes air density at 1.194 kg/m&sup3;).

    <h3>15. Where to point the arm (section 6)</h3>
    Section 6 answers "if I want the ball to finish on this course, where does the arm have to be pointed" with a single point.
    Pick the <b>target course (&#9679;)</b> with the 3&times;3 buttons, the height and horizontal sliders, or by clicking the figure,
    and it solves the delivery that actually gets there, then draws <b>where that same delivery would land with no spin (&#9675;)</b> with an arrow between them.
    The gap between &#9675; and &#9679; <i>is</i> the break.
    <br><br>
    For example, to finish a 96 mph / 2400 rpm / 95% / 11:30 four-seam middle-middle (31 inches high),
    the arm points at <b>12 inches high, 5 inches to the glove side</b> — 19 inches below and 5 inches outside the target.
    A curveball to the same spot points at <b>50 inches high</b>, 18 inches <i>above</i> where you want it.
    The quick-reference table on the right gives all nine courses so you can take the numbers away with you.
    <br><br>
    <b>This offset is essentially the same wherever in the zone you aim.</b> Computed over 25 points, the variation stays under half an inch,
    because break is set by spin and velocity, not by where you aim. One vector per pitch type covers every course.
    Tick "Show the whole zone as a lattice" to see it.
    <br><br>
    <b>It is not a place a pitcher should look, though.</b>
    Real pitchers look at the spot they want the ball to finish, and the arm angle is something the body knows. Do not use it as a coaching cue.
    What it is worth is <b>a number for how far apart the arm directions are between pitch types</b>. Two pitches a long way apart cannot be thrown with the same arm action.
    <br><br>
    Switch to <b>"How it looks at the decision point"</b> and &#9675; becomes the actual point the pitch passes through 23.8 feet out.
    That is the hitter's world. Put it on "Whole arsenal" and pairs whose &#9675; overlap are a tunnel — exactly what sections 4 and 5 are hunting for.
    <br><br>
    <b>"Send this aim to section 2 and throw it"</b> loads the value into section 2's aim and throws it, for checking the figure against the simulation.
    <br><br>
    <b>On the viewpoint</b>: every figure in section 6 is drawn <b>looking from the batter's box toward the pitcher</b> — the direction the catcher, batter and umpire face,
    which is left-right reversed from a TV centre-field camera.
    "Batter's eye" uses exactly the same camera as section 2's batter view (behind the box, 4.9 ft eye height, offset 16 inches to the batter's side),
    so the zone looks slightly trapezoidal. Use it when comparing against the section 2 screen.
    "Straight on" is an orthographic view with no perspective, which is the accurate one for reading distances off the picture. Both face the same way.

    <h3>About the model</h3>
    A numerical integration including drag (Cd&asymp;0.35) and Magnus force. Section 1 measures break over roughly a 50-foot span while section 2 uses the whole flight from release to the front of the plate,
    so section 2's offsets come out a little larger than section 1's break figures. The spin axis is held constant through the flight.
    Real pitches also carry effects this model leaves out, such as seam-shifted wake and precession of the gyro component.
    The strike zone is 17 inches wide (the plate) and 19.7&ndash;43.3 inches high, and a pitch counts as a strike if the ball's 1.45-inch radius catches any part of it.

